  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Substituting 5–10% of the Zr sublattice with Hf will enable ZrB2 densification at approximately 1700 °C.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Hf substitution will facilitate formation of an ultrafine HfO2 protective scale during high-temperature service.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The HfO2 scale will preferentially form at grain boundaries.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The HfO2 scale will improve oxidation resistance relative to conventional high-temperature ZrB2 and prior lower-temperature specimens.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The HfO2 scale will improve resistance to liquid plating-metal corrosion relative to conventional high-temperature ZrB2 and prior lower-temperature specimens.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Spark plasma sintering at approximately 1700 °C under an inert atmosphere can produce a homogeneous, single-phase (Zr1−xHfx)B2 solid solution.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "High-temperature service will induce slight Hf segregation from the homogeneous solid solution.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Segregated Hf will form nanoscale HfO2 layers at grain boundaries.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Hf substitution increases defect concentration in (Zr,Hf)B2.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Hf substitution increases diffusion rates during sintering.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Hf substitution lowers the activation energy for sintering.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The increased defect concentration and diffusion rates permit densification at a lower temperature.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "HfO2 is thermodynamically stable under the proposed high-temperature oxidative or liquid-metal service conditions.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The HfO2 formed during service acts as a barrier against further degradation.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Hf-substituted material will achieve full densification at 1700 °C.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Hf-substituted material will have mechanical strength comparable or superior to conventional ZrB2.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Lower-temperature ZrB2 variants without Hf lack protective oxide formation.",
      "field": "baseline",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proposed protection is limited to service conditions in which formation of a stable HfO2 scale is thermodynamically favorable.",
